1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
When building agents, they're often working on longer-running tasks.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:12,000
For these types of tasks, there are two really important concepts, persistence and streaming.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Persistence lets you keep around the state of an agent at a particular point in time.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,000
This can let you go back to that state and resume in that state in future interactions.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,000
This is really important for long-running applications.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Likewise, with streaming, you can emit a list of signals of what's going on at that exact moment.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,000
So for long-running applications, you know exactly what the agent is doing.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Let's see these concepts in action.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:47,000
So to get started, let's create our agent as we did before.

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
We'll load in the appropriate environment variables.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
We'll make the necessary imports.

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
We'll create our Tavilly Search Tool again.

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,000
We'll create our agent state.

14
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
And finally, we'll create our agent again.

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Now, we're going to add in persistence.

16
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:19,000
In order to deal with persistence, we've added the concept of a checkpointer into lane graph.

17
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
A checkpointer basically checkpoints the state after and between every node.

18
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
To add in persistence for this agent, what we'll do is we'll use a SQLite saver.

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,000
So this is a really simple checkpointer that we've added that uses SQLite, a built-in database under the hood.

20
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
And we'll just use the in-memory database.

21
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
So if we refresh this notebook, it'll disappear.

22
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
But you can easily connect this to an external database.

23
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Or we also have other check pointers that use redis and postgres and other more persistent databases like that.

24
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Once we initialize this checkpointer, the way that we can use it is we're going to pass it in to graph.compile.

25
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
So in order to make this easy, let's add another parameter to agent that is check pointer.

26
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
And then we're just going to pass checkpointer equals check pointer right here.

27
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,000
And we've modified our agent.

28
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,000
This is all we're going to need to do.

29
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,000
We can now create our agent.

30
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
And we're going to pass in checkpointer equals memory and remember memories the object that we initialized above.

31
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
When we use our agent now, we're also going to add the concept of streaming.

32
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,000
And there's two things that we might care about streaming.

33
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
First, we might care about streaming the individual messages.

34
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
So this would be the AI message that determines what action to take.

35
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,000
And then the observation message that represents the result of taking that action.

36
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
The second thing we might care about streaming is tokens.

37
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
So for each token of the LLM call, we might want to stream the output.

38
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
To begin, we're just going to start by streaming only the messages.

39
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
We'll do the tokens later on in the lesson.

40
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,000
So we're going to create our human message.

41
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
What is the weather in SF?

42
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
This is the one we ran before.

43
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
We're now going to add this concept of a thread config.

44
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
So this will be used to keep track of different threads inside the persistent checkpointer.

45
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,000
This will allow us to have multiple conversations going on at the same time.

46
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
This is really needed for production applications where you generally have many users.

47
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
This thread config is simply a dictionary with a configurable key.

48
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
And as part of that, we have a thread ID.

49
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
And we can set that equal to any string.

50
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Here, we're going to set that equal to one.

51
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:51,000
We're now going to call the graph not within Vogue, but with stream.

52
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
We're going to pass in the same messages dictionary.

53
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
And we're also going to pass in this thread config as a second parameter there.

54
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
We're then going to get back a stream of events.

55
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,000
These events represent updates to that state over time.

56
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Because we know our state only has one key, the message is key.

57
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
We're just going to loop through it and print that out.

58
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
So let's run this and see what happens.

59
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,000
We can see that we get back a stream of results.

60
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,000
First, we get back an AI message.

61
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
This is the first result from the language model.

62
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
And it's telling us to call to Ville.

63
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Next, we get back a tool message.

64
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
This is the result of calling to Ville.

65
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,000
And it has the results from the search.

66
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
And finally, we get back a third AI message.

67
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
This is the final result from the LLM answering our question.

68
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:48,000
With this stream method, we get back all of these intermediate results.

69
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
And we have really good visibility into what exactly is going on.

70
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Let's now call it with another message.

71
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
This time, we're going to say what about an LA?

72
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
So this is continuing the same conversation that we had before.

73
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
It's asking a follow-up question.

74
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
We don't say anything explicitly about the weather.

75
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
But based on it being a conversation,

76
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:13,000
we would expect it to realize that we're asking about the weather here.

77
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,000
In order to make sure that we're continuing from that same point,

78
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
we're passing in the same thread ID here.

79
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
If we run this,

80
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:27,000
we can see that it returns first a function call

81
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,000
where it's looking for current weather in Los Angeles.

82
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Again, it's knowing that we asked about weather

83
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:38,000
because it has this persistence from the checkpointer.

84
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,000
We can then see that it's getting back results from to Ville.

85
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,000
And finally, that is responding with an AI message

86
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:54,000
that says the current weather in Los Angeles is blah, blah, blah.

87
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,000
We can call this yet again using the same thread ID

88
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
with the message which is warmer.

89
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
Here, it has access to the full history.

90
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,000
So it can accurately respond.

91
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Los Angeles is currently warmer than San Francisco.

92
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Just to demonstrate the importance of this thread ID,

93
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
let's change this to be two.

94
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,000
If we run this now,

95
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
we can see that the language model is really confused.

96
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Can you please specify the two or more items

97
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
you are comparing to determine which is warmer?

98
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
That's because it doesn't have access to any history.

99
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
And that's because we're using a separate thread ID.

100
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
So we've covered the importance of persistence

101
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
and we've showed how you can stream events.

102
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
But what about streaming tokens themselves?

103
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:48,000
For that, we're going to want to use the aStreamEvents method

104
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:53,000
that comes on all lane chain and lane graph objects.

105
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
AStreamEvents is an asynchronous method

106
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:02,000
which means that we're going to need to use an async checkpointer.

107
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:06,000
In order to do this, we can import async SQLiteSaver

108
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
and pass that to the agent.

109
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
This is very similar to before.

110
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
It's just swapping out a synchronous SQLiteSaver

111
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
with an async SQLiteSaver.

112
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:22,000
This allows us to use async methods on the graph.

113
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
We'll use a new thread ID.

114
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
So this will start the conversation from fresh.

115
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
We're also going to be iterating

116
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
over a different type of event.

117
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:34,000
These events represent updates from the underlying stream.

118
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
What we want to do is we want to look for events

119
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,000
that correspond to new tokens.

120
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,000
These kind of events are called onChapModelStream.

121
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
When we see these events happening,

122
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
we want to get the content and print it out.

123
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
And we'll print it out with this pipe delimiter.

124
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
When we run this, we should see it streaming real time

125
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
into the screen.

126
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:07,000
So we can see a few things here.

127
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,000
First, we can see that it called the function under the hood.

128
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:14,000
The reason it didn't stream out anything there

129
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:17,000
is there actually was no content to stream.

130
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
It was just a function call.

131
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
But then we can see that when it did get to the final response

132
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:24,000
and it is returning a final answer,

133
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,000
we stream out those tokens one at a time.

134
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:30,000
We can see that we've got this little funny pipe delimiter here.

135
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
But we could easily remove that in our production application

136
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
if we wanted to.

137
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
So that's it for persistence and streaming.

138
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Pretty simple to get started with.

139
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
But really powerful for building production applications.

140
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
You're going to want your agents to be able to have

141
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
multiple conversations at the same time

142
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
and have a concept of memory so they can resume those conversations.

143
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
And you're also going to want them to be able to stream

144
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
both the final tokens,

145
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
but also all of the messages that came before.

146
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:04,000
Persistence is also really important for enabling human

147
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
and the loop type interactions.

148
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:10,000
And that's exactly what we're going to cover in the next lesson.
